{"id": "2026-10-30-news-switzerland-and-china-sign-upgraded-trade-deal", "date": "2026-10-30", "stream": "news", "headline": "Switzerland and China sign upgraded trade deal", "summary": "The upgraded Switzerland-China free trade agreement and side deals on labour and research data were signed in Beijing on 30 October during Guy Parmelin's state visit.", "url": "https://www.admin.ch/fr/newnsb/Nb4J2LzVCEaA", "source_domain": "admin.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 3, "display_body": "President Guy Parmelin and Chinese President Xi Jinping on Friday 30 October oversaw the signing in Beijing of the upgraded Switzerland–China free trade agreement, the Federal Council said. The update modernises the 2014 deal, which was the first free trade agreement China concluded with a European country. According to the government, it improves access to the Chinese market for Swiss goods and investment, and adds a legally binding chapter on trade and sustainable development covering environmental and labour rules, which the government says is a first in any Chinese free trade agreement. Two side agreements were signed as well: one on labour and employment cooperation and one that sets legal rules for exchanging research data between Swiss and Chinese researchers. The government says the two presidents also discussed human rights, noting that the latest session of the bilateral human-rights dialogue took place in Bern on 19–20 October. Parmelin's visit continues until Saturday 31 October, with meetings planned with China's commerce, labour and education ministers.", "why": "China is Switzerland's third-largest trading partner after the EU and the US. The deal widens a trade channel for Swiss exporters after the US imposed steep tariffs on Swiss goods in 2025. The communiqué does not say how the new labour and environment chapter will be enforced.", "thread_id": "2026-10-30-news-switzerland-and-china-sign-upgraded-trade-deal", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-30", "event_date": "2026-10-30", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Switzerland", "China", "Guy Parmelin", "Xi Jinping"]}
{"id": "2026-10-30-news-snb-books-44-2-billion-profit-to-september", "date": "2026-10-30", "stream": "news", "headline": "SNB books 44.2 billion profit to September", "summary": "The SNB reported a CHF 44.2 billion profit for the first nine months of 2026, mainly from foreign-currency positions.", "url": "https://www.snb.ch/en/publications/communication/press-releases/2026/pre_20261030", "source_domain": "snb.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The Swiss National Bank reported a profit of 44.2 billion francs for the first nine months of 2026 in interim results published on Friday 30 October. Almost all of it, 43.3 billion, came from its foreign-currency investments. Exchange-rate gains contributed 29.8 billion and equities 23.7 billion, while bonds lost 22.6 billion as interest rates rose worldwide. Gold, after a loss at mid-year, ended September with a valuation gain of 1.2 billion. The SNB repeats that its result depends largely on the gold, currency and capital markets, and that \"only provisional conclusions are possible\" about the full year.", "why": "the annual result decides whether the Confederation and the cantons receive a profit distribution next spring. That decision is still three months of market moves away, so the nine-month figure does not guarantee a payout.", "thread_id": "2026-10-30-news-snb-books-44-2-billion-profit-to-september", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-30", "event_date": "2026-10-30", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Swiss National Bank"]}
{"id": "2026-10-30-news-swiss-chips-still-found-in-russian-weapons", "date": "2026-10-30", "stream": "news", "headline": "Swiss chips still found in Russian weapons", "summary": "Ukraine's HUR database lists components from eleven Swiss-based manufacturers in new Russian drones and missiles; Kyiv urges tougher Swiss enforcement.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/schweiz/drohnen-krieg-in-der-ukraine-schweizer-bauteile-auch-in-neuen-russischen-waffen-entdeckt", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["switzerland", "geopolitics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Electronic parts from eleven Swiss-based manufacturers keep turning up in new Russian weapons, including the jet-powered Geran-4 attack drone and the \"Filin\" cruise missile, according to a database of Ukraine's military intelligence agency HUR reported by SRF on Friday 30 October. The parts are mass-produced civilian components such as microcontrollers, satellite-navigation processors, voltage converters and memory chips. They are not classed as war material, but the EU and G7 put them on a common sanctions list that Switzerland has adopted, so selling them to Russia is banned. Vladyslav Vlasiuk, an adviser to Ukraine's president, says Switzerland is doing too little and wants prosecutors brought in and export controls strengthened. The State Secretariat for Economic Affairs (SECO) told SRF that most of the parts are not made in Switzerland, so no exports from Switzerland take place, and that companies must contractually bar re-export to Russia. The firms SRF contacted, including STMicroelectronics (Plan-les-Ouates, GE) and u-blox (Thalwil, ZH), say they stopped all business with Russia in 2022.", "why": "the parts reach Russia through distributors and middlemen in other countries, not through direct sales. That means the dispute is about how much responsibility a Swiss-headquartered firm has for its supply chain, not about breaking the export ban. Vlasiuk notes that parts from the Dutch chipmaker NXP have almost disappeared from Russian weapons, which suggests firms can do more.", "thread_id": "2026-10-30-news-swiss-chips-still-found-in-russian-weapons", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-30", "event_date": "2026-10-30", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Switzerland", "Russia", "Ukraine", "SECO", "STMicroelectronics"]}
{"id": "2026-10-30-news-junge-tat-leaders-get-suspended-sentences", "date": "2026-10-30", "stream": "news", "headline": "Junge Tat leaders get suspended sentences", "summary": "Zurich district court gave two Junge Tat leaders 12-month suspended sentences for discrimination and incitement to hatred over 2022-2024 actions.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/schweiz/drag-lesung-gestoert-mitglieder-der-jungen-tat-verurteilt", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "politics"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Zurich district court on Friday 30 October gave two leaders of the group Junge Tat 12-month suspended prison sentences, with four years' probation, plus fines they must pay, for actions in Zurich, Basel, Winterthur and St. Gallen between 2022 and 2024. The charges included discrimination, incitement to hatred, disrupting freedom of religion, trespass and breaching the explosives law. Among the incidents were the disruption of a Pride church service in Zurich in June 2022 and of a children's drag reading at the Tanzhaus in October 2022. Prosecutors had asked for 15 months, six of them to be served. Three other members received suspended fines and one woman was acquitted on all counts. The leaders, whom SRF and Le Temps describe as heading a far-right group, said they had acted within freedom of expression. The verdicts can be appealed to the Zurich high court.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-10-30-news-junge-tat-leaders-get-suspended-sentences", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-30", "event_date": "2026-10-30", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Junge Tat", "Zurich"]}
{"id": "2026-10-30-news-listeria-warning-widened-to-more-soft-cheeses", "date": "2026-10-30", "stream": "news", "headline": "Listeria warning widened to more soft cheeses", "summary": "The FSVO extended a listeria warning to more soft cheeses from Eberle Spezialitäten and Strähl Käse sold at Migros, Coop and Volg.", "url": "https://www.admin.ch/fr/newnsb/9ELyAwEjEEWJ", "source_domain": "admin.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["switzerland", "health"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "The federal food safety office (FSVO) on Thursday 29 October extended its listeria warning to more soft cheeses made by Eberle Spezialitäten AG and Strähl Käse AG and sold at Migros, Coop, Volg and other shops. Its first warning, on 27 October, covered only mini-bries from Eberle. Both producers have withdrawn the products and ordered a recall, and the office advises against eating them. Listeria usually causes mild illness or none, but it can be dangerous for newborns, older people, pregnant women and people with weakened immune systems. The list of affected cheeses is in a PDF attached to the warning.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-10-30-news-listeria-warning-widened-to-more-soft-cheeses", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-30", "event_date": "2026-10-29", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["FSVO", "Migros", "Coop"]}
{"id": "2026-10-30-news-committee-backs-digital-sovereignty-rule-for-pro", "date": "2026-10-30", "stream": "news", "headline": "Committee backs digital-sovereignty rule for procurement", "summary": "A National Council committee voted 16-5 for a motion imposing graded digital-sovereignty requirements on federal IT procurement.", "url": "https://www.letemps.ch/articles/un-tabou-vacille-la-suisse-pourrait-activer-la-preference-nationale-pour-ses-acquisitions-numeriques", "source_domain": "letemps.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["switzerland", "tech"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "The National Council's economic affairs committee voted 16 to 5, with 3 abstentions, for a motion that would require federal IT, cloud and AI purchases to meet \"verifiable and measurable\" digital-sovereignty requirements, Le Temps reported on Friday 30 October. The motion, pushed by Vaud Green MP Sophie Michaud Gigon, does not require Swiss suppliers for every purchase. Instead, the requirements would be graded by how sensitive the data involved is. Le Temps describes it as opening the door to a form of national preference in public procurement, which until recently was taboo. The committee met on Monday 26 and Tuesday 27 October, and the motion still has to pass both chambers.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-10-30-news-committee-backs-digital-sovereignty-rule-for-pro", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-30", "event_date": "2026-10-27", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["National Council", "Sophie Michaud Gigon"]}
{"id": "2026-10-30-news-israel-s-final-count-confirms-hung-knesset", "date": "2026-10-30", "stream": "news", "headline": "Israel's final count confirms hung Knesset", "summary": "Final results of Israel's 27 October election give Netanyahu's bloc 53 seats and Eisenkot's 48, short of the 61 needed; Arab-led lists won 19.", "url": "https://www.rfi.fr/fr/moyen-orient/20261030-l%C3%A9gislatives-en-isra%C3%ABl-le-camp-de-benyamin-netanyahu-en-t%C3%AAte-mais-sans-majorit%C3%A9", "source_domain": "rfi.fr", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics", "politics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Final results of Israel's election of Tuesday 27 October, published overnight into Friday 30 October, confirm a hung Knesset: Benjamin Netanyahu's outgoing coalition has 53 of 120 seats and the opposition bloc led by Gadi Eisenkot has 48.", "why": "neither bloc can govern without either a unity deal or support from the Arab-led parties, and the right-wing parties in Eisenkot's own bloc may resist relying on the latter. Which government emerges will largely decide whether Israel's policy in Gaza continues on its current course.", "thread_id": "2026-10-30-news-israel-s-final-count-confirms-hung-knesset", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-30", "event_date": "2026-10-27", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Israel", "Benjamin Netanyahu", "Gadi Eisenkot", "Knesset", "Ra'am"]}
{"id": "2026-10-30-news-lagarde-to-leave-ecb-in-february", "date": "2026-10-30", "stream": "news", "headline": "Lagarde to leave ECB in February", "summary": "Christine Lagarde will step down as ECB president on 7 February 2027; the ECB kept rates unchanged on 29 October.", "url": "https://www.ecb.europa.eu/press/pr/date/2026/html/ecb.pr261029~abbb6dc64b.en.html", "source_domain": "ecb.europa.eu", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["economy"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "European Central Bank President Christine Lagarde told the Governing Council on Thursday 29 October that she will step down on 7 February 2027, about nine months before her term ends. The ECB gave no reason. Her eight-year term began on 1 November 2019. European Council President António Costa said the process to choose her successor would start immediately. The successor is appointed by the European Council under Article 283 of the EU treaty. DW reports speculation that the early exit is meant to let EU leaders choose the next president before France's presidential election next spring. On the same day the ECB left its three key interest rates unchanged. It warned that the continuing rise in energy prices poses upside risks to inflation and downside risks to growth, and that the financing conditions it watches have tightened.", "why": "the ECB's choices on euro rates drive the franc's exchange rate and, through it, the Swiss National Bank's own policy. A leadership race held during an energy shock adds uncertainty for the euro area, Switzerland's largest export market.", "thread_id": "2026-10-30-news-lagarde-to-leave-ecb-in-february", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-30", "event_date": "2026-10-29", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["European Central Bank", "Christine Lagarde", "António Costa"]}
{"id": "2026-10-30-news-french-economy-contracts-in-third-quarter", "date": "2026-10-30", "stream": "news", "headline": "French economy contracts in third quarter", "summary": "INSEE's first estimate shows French GDP down 0.1% in Q3 2026 after 0.0% in Q2, with October inflation at 3.4%.", "url": "https://www.insee.fr/fr/statistiques/9111036", "source_domain": "insee.fr", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR", "new source"], "topics": ["economy"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "France's economy shrank by 0.1% in the third quarter, after zero growth in the second, according to the first estimate published by the statistics institute INSEE on Friday 30 October.", "why": "France is one of Switzerland's main trading partners. Shrinking output combined with rising inflation in the eurozone's second-largest economy narrows the room for both the government's budget plans and the ECB.", "thread_id": "2026-10-30-news-french-economy-contracts-in-third-quarter", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-30", "event_date": "2026-10-30", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["France", "INSEE", "Roland Lescure"]}
{"id": "2026-10-30-news-oleshky-aid-and-evacuation-mission-begins", "date": "2026-10-30", "stream": "news", "headline": "Oleshky aid and evacuation mission begins", "summary": "A joint Russian-Ukrainian humanitarian mission began delivering aid and offering evacuation in occupied Oleshky on 30 October under a local ceasefire.", "url": "https://www.ukrinform.net/rubric-ato/4179663-humanitarian-aid-being-delivered-to-oleshky-25-buses-with-750-seats-ready-for-evacuation-lubinets.html", "source_domain": "ukrinform.net", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["new source"], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Russian and Ukrainian officials began a joint humanitarian mission on Friday 30 October in Oleshky, a front-line town in the Russian-occupied part of Kherson region, delivering aid and offering evacuation, Ukraine's human-rights commissioner Dmytro Lubinets said.", "why": "it is a rare jointly run Russian–Ukrainian civilian operation across the front line. Whether the local ceasefire holds is an early test before the trilateral talks with the US expected in November.", "thread_id": "2026-10-30-news-oleshky-aid-and-evacuation-mission-begins", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-30", "event_date": "2026-10-30", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Ukraine", "Russia", "Oleshky", "Dmytro Lubinets"]}
